// ============================================================
// CHARACTER: Angelic Messengers (Two Men in White)
// ACT: Act IV
// CASE: The Ascension
// CASE ID: ascension
// SOURCE: js/act4_case.js -> NPC 'angelic_messengers'
// BIBLE REFERENCE: Acts 1:10-11
// ------------------------------------------------------------
// PROPHECIES FULFILLED IN THIS CASE:
//   - Acts 1:9-11
//     Gospel: Acts 1:9-11
//     Insight: The angels confirm the ascension and promise Christ's return in the same manner.
//   - Daniel 7:13-14
//     Gospel: Acts 1:9-11
//     Insight: The Son of Man's ascension to the Ancient of Days, fulfilling Daniel's vision of divine enthronement.
// ============================================================

-> start
=== start ===
We stood on the Mount of Olives, watching the cloud receive Him. The disciples stared upward, motionless, their faces a mixture of awe and confusion. They had seen the resurrected Lord for forty days, and now they had seen Him ascend. But they did not understand what to do next.
* [Why did you appear?] -> purpose
* [What did you say to them?] -> message
=== purpose ===
He had told them to wait in Jerusalem for the promise of the Father. They had heard the words, but they did not grasp the weight of them. They were still asking, 'Lord, are you at this time going to restore the kingdom to Israel?' They needed a reset. A divine interruption to shift their gaze from the sky to the world.
* [What was the message?] -> message
=== message ===
'Men of Galilee, why do you stand gazing into heaven? This same Jesus, who has been taken from you into heaven, will come back in the same way you have seen him go into heaven.' It was not a greeting. It was a commission wrapped in a promise. The ascension was not an ending — it was a departure for a return.
* [Did they believe you?] -> belief
=== belief ===
Some looked at each other. Others looked back at the empty sky. But a few — the ones who had touched the wounds, who had eaten with Him, who had heard the voice — they understood. The One who left would return. And until then, they had work to do.
* [What is the promise?] -> promise
=== promise ===
The promise is simple: He will come again. Not as a servant in humility, but as King in glory. Not to die, but to judge. Not to bless and then ascend, but to reign forever. The ascension is the guarantee. The cloud is the pattern. The return is certain.
* [What should they do now?] -> closing
=== closing ===
They went back to Jerusalem. They gathered in the upper room. They prayed. They waited. And when the Spirit came, they remembered our words. The commission became a movement. The promise became a hope that changed the world. He is coming back. Be ready.
-> DONE
